YAAP -- Chang (Vegan/Weaponless/Genoless/Polyless/Body-Armorless) --
110 max HP, -22 AC


I haven't posted a YAAP for a long time, but I this is my first NAO
ascension, so I'm writing a YAAP for it.

There are many things this Monk was able to do, that I haven't been
able to do in my previous games.  This is my first Vegan game, as my
other Monks wanted to eat floating eyes and black dragons.  This is my
first Weaponless game, as my other Monks accidentally hit monsters with
a pick-axe.  This is my first Genoless game, as my other Monks wanted
to genocide all the mind flayers and all the liches.

Genoless ended up being much easier than I thought it would be.  I was
previously paranoid that mind flayers would get summoned next to me,
and would attack me before I could defend myself.  It turns out that
when monsters are summoned, the player always has at least 1 turn to
burn Elbereth before the summoned monsters start attacking.  So as long
as I had wands of fire, the mind flayers weren't a threat.  The
self-teleporting Liches ended up being easy when I figured out that I
can stand next to the up-stairs, teleport the Lich off of the stairs,
then stand on the stairs.

This is my first NAO game, and also my first game that had bones
levels.  I found out that bone piles like to be filled with useful
stuff.  This was the strangest game I've played so far, because it
contained far more magic markers and wishing wands than I've ever seen
before.  Most of my wishes were completely unnecessary, as I was doing
stuff like wishing for things that were already in the game, because I
was too lazy to walk across the dungeon to retrieve items I left
behind.  I also wasted wishes on things like paralysis potions, because
I really didn't have anything that I needed.

Looking back at the game, it wouldn't have been possible to go
wishless, because I don't think any magic resistance items were
randomly generated.  I still think genoless/polyless/wishless is only
easily done by a Wizard that gets the amulet of reflection in Sokoban.
Unfortunately, Wizards take so much real-time to play, that I rarely
play them.


